# Accounts, permissions and shared settings

Say whose experience a setting changes before the person changes it, and show whether they are allowed to. Keep Home Assistant's own owner, administrator and other user roles. civica changes appearance. It is not an authorisation system.

This page is contract HA-CTRL-013. Its examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or change a setting.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-013-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

Put the scope next to the control. Check what the installed version actually does before you write it.

| Scope | What to explain |
| --- | --- |
| Account | Name the signed-in account's preference. Check whether it follows that account to other browsers and apps. |
| Browser or mobile app | Say it applies to this client only, and what is lost on sign-out or when local data is removed. |
| Dashboard | Say which saved dashboard changes and who can view it under native rules. Hiding a dashboard does not restrict access to an entity. |
| Installation | Name the system-wide effect, the native role needed and anything that depends on it. |
| Authentication | Keep the native password, multi-factor authentication, session and token management. Never collect credentials in a theme control. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-013-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Keep a permitted edit, a pending save, a rejected request and a lost session apart. An enabled button does not prove the change is allowed. If permissions change while a form is open, show Home Assistant's rejection. Do not say the change was saved and do not reveal restricted data.

A user account and a person entity do different jobs. Changing a display name, changing a login, revoking a session and removing a tracked person are separate operations. Do not invent narrower permissions, such as “guest, one room only”, because a dashboard hides other cards.

On a shared device, say whether a change affects this browser or a reusable account. A “shared” label does not mean the account has less access in the backend. A restriction on where a person may sign in is not a room or device permission.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-013-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Put the scope before submission, for example “For this browser” or “For your account”, whichever the implementation really does. Use the settings labels Home Assistant shows in the current language.

Do not show an access token, password or recovery code in an example, screenshot or copyable snippet. State an authorisation error as a fact. Do not suggest that a different theme will fix it.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-013-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Keep the permission explanation readable when an action is unavailable. Do not rely on disabled styling alone to show the account's status. Native sign-in fields keep their password-manager, keyboard and assistive-technology behaviour. An expired session must lead to the native sign-in without a keyboard trap or a hidden focused form.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-013-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Use the native profile and authentication flows. Home Assistant separates profile preferences from settings kept only in the browser. Check the installed version, and how the civica companion stores each setting, before you claim a setting persists. These examples only review scope. They are not a user-management tool. Home Assistant stays the authority even if someone calls an action directly or opens a hidden route.

